From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nick Randolph <noreply@platform.uno>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: report which objects reference a condemned collectible
 ALC witness

The GC-handle classification added earlier answered its question in the
negative. Across a downstream WASM host's stranding repro -- repeated
collectible-ALC load/unload cycles with the forced-unload opt-in active --
eight complete unsaturated sweeps report every handle bucket at 0 (strong,
pinned and weak-track alike) while managers_in_scope equals live_witnesses
exactly on every cycle and never decays:

  cycle=1  managers=2  liveWitnesses=2  strong=0 pinned=0 weakTrack=0
  cycle=8  managers=16 liveWitnesses=16 strong=0 pinned=0 weakTrack=0

That rules out a GC handle DIRECTLY targeting a condemned object, which is
all the snapshot can ever see: its attribution is one hop, so a handle on a
container that merely references a witness is invisible to it. The witnesses
are nevertheless reachable, so the reference runs through the object graph --
and no amount of further handle classification can follow a graph.

This adds the reverse lookup: one bounded pass over the heap that reports,
for each condemned witness, the CLASS of the objects referencing it and the
field offset of the reference. It names the holder instead of narrowing the
search space.

  * sgen_scan_referrers_of_condemned (sgen-mono.c) mirrors the three regions
    mono_gc_walk_heap covers -- nursery, major heap, LOS -- with a scanner
    that tests each reference in place against the condemned set. It does not
    reuse mono_gc_walk_heap because that entry point buffers every reference
    of every object through a MonoGCReferences callback with no budget and no
    way for the callback to stop, the same shape problem
    sgen_gchandle_foreach_slot_bounded was added to solve for the handle
    tables; all this walk needs is one hash lookup per reference.
  * assembly-load-context.c owns the trigger, the condemned set and the
    report. A quiescence refusal is the only evidence a witness is still
    held, so a refusal RECORDS A REQUEST; the END OF THE NEXT MAJOR
    COLLECTION serves it, because a heap walk is only legal with the world
    stopped and a collection finished -- and only a MAJOR one establishes
    old-generation liveness. The condemned identity set is built by
    alc_condemned_set_collect, shared with the handle snapshot from the
    preceding patch, so both consumers derive the set the same way.
  * sgen_client_collection_end is a NEW sgen client hook -- declared in
    sgen/sgen-client.h, defined in sgen-mono.c, called from
    sgen_perform_collection_inner immediately before sgen_restart_world --
    that carries that call, under DISABLE_THREADS only. It forwards
    oldest_generation_collected, which is what lets the serving side hold a
    request back until the OLD generation has actually been collected.

THE HOOK'S CONTRACT lives on its declaration in sgen/sgen-client.h, stated
once for every future client rather than per call site, and it has two halves.

A COLLECTION-ONLY HOOK, because the restart hook is not one. sgen_restart_world
is reached from stop-the-worlds that never collect: sgen_get_total_allocated_bytes
(TRUE) -- the implementation of the perfectly ordinary public
GC.GetTotalAllocatedBytes (precise: true) -- and sgen_check_whole_heap_stw () in
verification mode both stop and restart the world having collected nothing, and
neither argument discriminates, since both pass generation 0 exactly as a nursery
collection does. sgen-stw.c's own "this is the wrong spot at we can STW for non
collection reasons" comment sits immediately above where the call used to be.

A MAJOR COLLECTION, NOT ANY COLLECTION, because reaching collection completion
is not the same as having collected the old generation:
sgen_is_object_alive_for_current_gen () short-circuits to TRUE for EVERY object
outside the nursery while sgen_current_collection_generation ==
GENERATION_NURSERY, so a minor completion has pruned no dead OLD ephemeron array
or key and swept no major block.

Both halves cost this surface the same thing: a scan served on an unswept heap
or after a nursery-only collection reports DEAD GARBAGE -- the dependent-edge
pass applies no liveness predicate of its own, precisely because it relies on
that pruning, and a promoted holder dropped after the refusal is still
enumerable by ITERATE_OBJECTS_SWEEP_ALL -- and spends the one-shot request, so
the real collection that follows has nothing left to answer. Both are false
positives on a surface whose whole value is that a negative result can be
trusted.

So the new hook is unreachable from a non-collection path by construction: it is
called only from sgen_perform_collection_inner, inside `if (stw)`, after the
collection and before the world restarts. A thin client hook rather than a direct
call out of sgen-gc.c, because sgen-gc.c is collector core and includes nothing
from metadata/ -- only sgen/ and utils/ -- so reaching loader-internals.h from
there would have inverted that layering. sgen-mono.c is the mono client of sgen,
it already includes a dozen metadata/ headers, and it is where every other
sgen_client_* entry point lives. And it forwards oldest_generation_collected, so
mono_alc_referrer_scan_at_gc_pause returns with the request STILL ARMED -- before
the clear -- unless GENERATION_OLD completed. A nursery collection that overflows
into a major one already reports GENERATION_OLD (sgen_perform_collection_inner
raises oldest_generation_collected to the overflow generation), so escalation
serves the request instead of dropping it.

Two regressions, one per half:

  * ForcedUnload_ReferrerScan_NonCollectionStopTheWorldDoesNotServeTheRequest:
    refuse, read kind 11, call GC.GetTotalAllocatedBytes (precise: true),
    require kind 11 UNCHANGED, then collect and require it to advance. Against
    the old call site the first assertion fails; against a hook that never fires
    the second does.
  * ForcedUnload_ReferrerScan_NurseryOnlyCollectionDoesNotServeTheRequest: arm,
    run GC.Collect (0, Forced, blocking: true), require kind 11 UNCHANGED, then
    collect fully and require it to advance. The gen-0-only step is VERIFIED
    rather than assumed -- GC.CollectionCount (1) is bracketed across it and
    must not move -- because sgen escalates on its own: sgen_gc_collect () runs
    a "Minor allowance" major collection right after the nursery one when the
    allowance is exceeded, and sgen_perform_collection_inner escalates to a
    "Minor overflow" major. Either would turn the discriminating step into a
    full collection and make the unchanged-gauge assertion VACUOUS, so
    escalation fails the test loudly instead of reading as a pass.

DOUBLE OPT-IN. MONO_WASM_ENABLE_ALC_UNLOAD does not arm this on its own; it
also needs MONO_WASM_ALC_UNLOAD_REFERRER_SCAN. Unlike the snapshot -- a
read-only gauge a caller chooses to poll -- this adds work to a collection
pause, so a flagged session that has not asked for referrers pays one load
and a branch. The regression asserts that gating in both directions.

TWO PASSES, because one is not exhaustive. The heap walk is descriptor-driven,
and sgen zeroes the GC descriptor bitmap for System.Runtime.Ephemeron
(object.c, "An Ephemeron cannot be marked by sgen"), so a
ConditionalWeakTable or DependentHandle value edge is invisible to it -- while
being a genuine root, since sgen_client_mark_ephemerons marks the value
whenever the key is alive. On mono that is not a corner case: DependentHandle
IS an Ephemeron[] ("Instead of dependent handles, mono uses arrays of
Ephemeron objects"), so every CWT goes through it. A witness held only that
way would have produced an unsaturated EMPTY report -- a false negative of
exactly the kind this surface exists to rule out.

The ephemeron pass runs FIRST and applies NO liveness predicate, both
deliberately.

No predicate, because one would be wrong here and is also unnecessary.
sgen_client_clear_unreachable_ephemerons already ran inside the finishing MAJOR
collection -- which is what the generation gate above guarantees, and why that
gate is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. It unlinked and freed nodes whose
array was dead and tombstoned every entry whose key was dead, clearing its
value, so the list is already pruned to exactly the live arrays and live-keyed
entries and skipping NULL/tombstone keys is the whole test needed. An earlier
revision mirrored the collector and called
sgen_is_object_alive_for_current_gen, which silently dropped live PROMOTED
entries: the major pass passes ITERATE_OBJECTS_SWEEP_ALL, marksweep's
sweep_block clears block->mark_words, and with
sgen_current_collection_generation already -1 that predicate falls through to
sgen_major_is_object_alive and reads the cleared bit.

First, because it shares the slot budget and running it last let a saturating
heap walk starve it completely -- silently reinstating the blind spot it exists
to close, in exactly the small-budget/large-heap case where it matters most.

TWO BUDGETS, and neither bounds the pause. object_budget caps how many objects
are ENTERED. reference_slot_budget caps the SET LOOKUPS, across every object
entered and the ephemeron pass, and it exists because the object budget bounded
nothing expensive: the per-object scan invokes its callback once per reference
-- once per ELEMENT of a vector or complex array -- so one large object[] cost
a single object unit while performing millions of lookups on the one suspended
thread. That is the same defect shape as bounding a handle walk on classified
hits instead of raw slots.

What the budgets do NOT cover, stated because "bounded" was previously claimed
too broadly: the slot enumeration WITHIN an object already entered cannot be
interrupted (sgen-scan-object.h expands to a loop this code cannot break out
of), the heap enumeration is unbounded, and ITERATE_OBJECTS_SWEEP_ALL forces a
lazily-swept major heap to finish sweeping, exactly as mono_gc_walk_heap does.
A scan costs that enumeration plus the intra-object slot loads of everything
entered plus at most reference_slot_budget lookups. Because the iterators
always finish, objects_visited is a TRUE total, and
objects_scanned/objects_visited, reference_slots_visited and `saturated`
together say how much of the heap an answer covers. Since none of that is a
pause bound, the summary line carries the measured elapsed_us as well, next to
both budgets -- `saturated` alone does not say which one tripped.

A referrer row is direct evidence. An EMPTY report is much weaker and means
anything only when the scan did not saturate -- and everything this surface
emits goes to the assembly trace, so it is invisible without --trace=asm at
info or finer. An empty console is therefore never evidence of anything, which
the contract block says in those words.

HALF THE PRECONDITION IS NOW ASSERTED, which the old call site made impossible:

  * STOPPED -- asserted. sgen_client_collection_end runs BEFORE
    sgen_restart_world clears world_is_stopped, so sgen_is_world_stopped () is
    TRUE there and the scan checks it. At the old site the flag had already been
    cleared, and a draft that asserted it there aborted the runtime on the first
    armed scan; the fix was the call site, not the assertion. It doubles as the
    tripwire against moving the call back.
  * FINISHED, AND A MAJOR ONE -- still not assertable, and recorded so neither
    assertion returns. sgen clears sgen_current_collection_generation at the end
    of the minor/major collection function, which runs before the hook, so it
    reads -1 here exactly as it does outside a collection, and nothing left at
    that point tells a minor completion from a major one either. What
    establishes both halves is the call site.

mono_gc_walk_heap has the identical contract and asserts nothing about the
world either; it states the requirement and relies on its callers.

Also worth flagging: taking alcs_lock inside a GC pause would be a real
hazard on a threads-enabled runtime, where mono_coop_mutex_lock does a
MONO_ENTER_GC_SAFE transition when contended. It is sound here only because
this surface is DISABLE_THREADS-only and every MonoCoopMutex operation is an
empty inline there. The reasoning is recorded at the call site rather than
left implicit, because it stops being true the moment someone builds this
with threads enabled.

OBSERVABILITY, so this can actually be tested and operated. The scan's other
output is trace text, so nothing could distinguish an armed scan from one that
never ran -- which is precisely how two wrong assertions survived a fully green
build. Five stat kinds now exist, 11 through 15: kind 11 counts scans that
completed a walk AND emitted their report (excluding the early return taken when
the condemned set has already emptied, so it cannot be earned by a scan that
never looked at the heap), kinds 12 and 13 report the last reported scan's total
and DEPENDENT edge counts, and kinds 14 and 15 its slot count and saturation
flag. Kind 13 is what makes the ephemeron blind spot testable at all. They are
independent gauges, not a tuple: read kind 11 around 12-15 to know which scan
they describe.

For an operator rather than a test, the trace now covers the whole life of a
request: one line when a refusal RECORDS one (the FALSE -> TRUE transition only,
since requests coalesce into a single flag, so a retry loop gets one line per
burst rather than one per attempt) and the summary plus rows when a major
collection SERVES it. Without the first, "never enabled", "never refused" and
"refused but no major collection followed" all look identical -- and kind 11 is
reachable only through the internal stats icall, i.e. from a test. A rejected
MONO_WASM_ALC_UNLOAD_REFERRER_SLOT_BUDGET is likewise warned about once, naming
the value and the budget that took effect, rather than being visible only as a
slots=%d/%d field nobody has the mask for.

That budget is also resolved from the REFUSAL, with the world running, and only
read from the cache inside the pause -- the g_getenv itself must not run on a
suspended thread, and both consumers of the value are pause-side.

FALSIFIABILITY. Four things were previously asserted only by comment, and are
now testable:

  * The slot budget is overridable DOWNWARD via
    MONO_WASM_ALC_UNLOAD_REFERRER_SLOT_BUDGET, clamped to the production value
    so an override can only ever shrink the walk. Without it saturation was
    unreachable from a test -- a test heap cannot produce two million reference
    slots -- so deleting the per-slot charge outright left every assertion
    passing.
  * Kinds 14 and 15 report the last scan's slot count and saturation flag, so
    the cutoff can be asserted rather than inferred.
  * Kinds 11-13 report scans reported, total edges and DEPENDENT edges, all as
    edge counts taken from the scan itself rather than from the described rows,
    so the description caps can never make a found edge read as absent.
  * The ORDINARY, descriptor-driven edge -- every referrer that is not a
    ConditionalWeakTable value -- is asserted directly:
    ForcedUnload_ReferrerScan_LargeReferenceArrayStillReports keeps a small
    nursery-resident object[] holding the condemned wrapper and requires kind 12
    minus kind 13 to be at least 1. Every other referrer assertion is satisfied
    by the ephemeron pass alone, so deleting the condemned-set lookup from
    HANDLE_PTR left every leg green.

Four legs, and each covers something the others cannot:

  * ST flag OFF -- feature inactive.
  * ST flag ON + scan armed + lowered slot budget -- the only leg that executes
    this code. Asserts kind 11 advances after a refusal, that a non-collection
    stop-the-world does NOT advance it while the collection that follows does,
    that a CWT dependent edge onto the condemned wrapper is reported (with the
    table, key and array PROMOTED first, which is the configuration the invalid
    liveness predicate dropped), that a VERIFIED generation-0-only collection
    does NOT advance it while the full collection that follows does, that a
    reference array sized from the lowered budget crosses the cutoff exactly at
    it, and that an ordinary reference edge is reported at all.
  * ST flag ON + scan OFF -- new. The feature is active so refusals are real,
    and the scan must still do nothing. Nothing asserted this before: flag OFF
    cannot (no refusal is possible) and MT cannot (the surface is compiled
    out), so deleting the second opt-in -- making every flagged session pay for
    a heap walk in every pause -- would have stayed green everywhere.
  * MT -- the surface is compiled out and rejection is proved.

The loader sentinel's scan dimension REQUIRES all FIVE referrer tests present
and passed on both ST feature-ON legs, so deleting one cannot read as green. It
once returned OK with every one of those names absent.

Each test drives its context TERMINAL afterwards. That is not tidiness: this
suite has a standing invariant that no test leaves a context pending -- stated
in Unload_DroppedRootsWithoutAnyForcedAttempt's own comment and asserted
through PendingUnloadsGauge -- and a first revision sustained its refusals past
the end of the test, failing two OTHER tests. The refusal half of each now
lives in its own non-inlined frame so the probe, the CWT and the wide array are
unreachable before teardown, which a local in the test's frame would not
reliably be given the interpreter scans its live slot region conservatively.

Temporary diagnostic scaffolding on the same terms and the same tracking
issue (#176) as the snapshot it complements: it retires when the residual
root is found and fixed, or when the stop-rule fires.
